/** \addtogroup area_graph */
/** \defgroup theory_graph Theory of graphs
 \ingroup area_graph

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/graph` realize the GRAPH of chapter 9 of
 the book and the algorithms of chapters 9, 11, 13, 14 and 15 that work on it,
 i.e., the visits, the shortest paths, the minimum spanning trees, the maximum
 flows and a few problems that reduce to them. This page collects what the
 headers take for granted: the definitions and the notation shared by all of
 them (see \ref theory_graph_notation), the representations and the costs of
 their operations (see \ref theory_graph_repr), and, for each family of
 algorithms, the property on which its correctness rests and the argument
 behind its bound (from \ref theory_graph_visits to \ref theory_graph_euler).
 The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the
 comment of its function, together with the section of the book, or the paper,
 that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_graph_notation Definitions and notation

 A graph \f$ G = ( V , E ) \f$ has a set \f$ V \f$ of \f$ n \f$ nodes and a set
 \f$ E \f$ of \f$ m \f$ edges; an edge of an <em>oriented</em> graph is an
 ordered pair \f$ ( u , v ) \f$, which leaves \f$ u \f$ and enters \f$ v \f$,
 and an edge of an <em>unoriented</em> graph is an unordered pair
 \f$ [ u , v ] \f$, which the library stores as the two pairs \f$ ( u , v ) \f$
 and \f$ ( v , u ) \f$ (section 9.2 of the book). There are no loops
 \f$ ( u , u ) \f$, and hence \f$ 0 \le m \le n ( n - 1 ) \f$; a graph is said
 <em>sparse</em> when \f$ m \f$ is of the order of \f$ n \f$ and <em>dense</em>
 when it is of the order of \f$ n^2 \f$, which is the distinction that decides
 between the representations (see \ref theory_graph_repr). The nodes are named
 by the integers in \f$ [ 0 , \mathrm{bound}() ) \f$, a deleted node leaving
 its name unused, so that the vectors indexed by the nodes have
 \f$ \mathrm{bound}() \ge n \f$ places.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{adj}( u ) \f$</td>
     <td>the nodes \f$ v \f$ with \f$ ( u , v ) \in E \f$, the SET of the
         book; \f$ d( u ) = | \mathrm{adj}( u ) | \f$ is the (out)degree of
         \f$ u \f$, and \f$ \sum_u d( u ) = m \f$ (\f$ 2 m \f$ for an
         unoriented graph)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ w( u , v ) \f$</td>
     <td>the weight, or cost, or length, of the edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$, of
         the type <tt>W</tt> of the graph</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \delta( r , v ) \f$, \f$ d[ v ] \f$</td>
     <td>the distance from \f$ r \f$ to \f$ v \f$ (the fewest edges, or the
         least total cost, of a path), and the value that an algorithm
         keeps for it; \f$ \infty \f$ (<tt>INF</tt>, <tt>infinity()</tt>)
         when \f$ v \f$ is not reachable</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ p[ v ] \f$, \f$ T[ v ] \f$</td>
     <td>the parent of \f$ v \f$ in a tree rooted at the source, as a vector
         of parents, <tt>NIL</tt> for the root and the nodes not
         reached</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{dt}[ u ] \f$, \f$ \mathit{ft}[ u ] \f$</td>
     <td>the discovery and the finish times of \f$ u \f$ in a visit in
         depth, on one clock that each event advances by 1</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c( u , v ) \f$, \f$ f( u , v ) \f$,
         \f$ c_f( u , v ) \f$</td>
     <td>the capacity of an edge of a network, the flow on it, and the
         residual capacity \f$ c( u , v ) - f( u , v ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ ( S , V \setminus S ) \f$</td>
     <td>a cut, the edges with one end in \f$ S \f$ and the other out of
         it</td></tr>
 </table>

 A <em>path</em> from \f$ u \f$ to \f$ v \f$ is a sequence of nodes
 \f$ u = x_0 , x_1 , \ldots , x_k = v \f$ with
 \f$ ( x_{i-1} , x_i ) \in E \f$; it is <em>simple</em> if its nodes are
 distinct, and it is a <em>cycle</em> if \f$ k \ge 1 \f$ and \f$ u = v \f$
 (\f$ k \ge 3 \f$ for an unoriented graph). A graph with no cycle is
 <em>acyclic</em> (a DAG if oriented, a forest if not, a free tree if it is
 also connected). The nodes reachable from one another, the orientation
 ignored, form the <em>connected components</em>, and those reachable from
 one another along the orientation the <em>strongly connected
 components</em>.

 \section theory_graph_repr Representations and their costs

 The library has five realizations of the GRAPH, which differ in the space and
 in the time of the operations. The algorithms of the area are written on the
 concept `Graph` of `Graph.h`, i.e., on `V()`, `adj( u )` and
 `forEachAdj( u , f )`, and their bounds hold on lists or vectors of adjacency,
 where the scan of \f$ \mathrm{adj}( u ) \f$ costs
 \f$ \Theta( d( u ) + 1 ) \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of the GRAPH and the cost of their operations
          (sections 9.3 and 9.4 of the book)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Space</th><th><tt>hasEdge( u , v )</tt></th>
     <th>scan of <tt>adj( u )</tt></th><th>insert or delete an edge</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MatrixGraph</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ListGraph</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( d( u ) + 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArrayGraph</tt>, <tt>StaticGraph</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$ (\f$ O( \log d( u ) ) \f$ on <tt>StaticGraph</tt>,
         whose rows are sorted)</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( d( u ) + 1 ) \f$, one contiguous run</td>
     <td>none, the graph is static</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PointerGraph</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( d( u ) + 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 A matrix is usually preferable on the dense graphs and when `hasEdge()`
 dominates (e.g., `universalSink()` of `Properties.h` reads at most
 \f$ 3 ( n - 1 ) \f$ entries of it); on a sparse graph a visit costs
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ on the matrix against \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$ on the
 lists, since each row is read whole. Among the static realizations,
 `StaticGraph` stores each arc next to its weight and sorts the arcs of each
 node by head, so that a visit reads the memory forward (cf. `StaticGraph.h`);
 both are built from a list of edges by counting sort in
 \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$, since the keys are the names of the nodes, i.e.,
 integers in \f$ [ 0 , n ) \f$.

 \section theory_graph_visits Visits

 The generic visit of section 9.5 (`genericVisit()` of `Visit.h`) keeps the
 reached nodes \f$ R \f$, the open ones \f$ Q \subseteq R \f$ and a tree
 \f$ B \f$: at each step it takes a node \f$ j \f$ of \f$ Q \f$ and either
 reaches a node \f$ k \in \mathrm{adj}( j ) \setminus R \f$, adding
 \f$ ( j , k ) \f$ to \f$ B \f$, or closes \f$ j \f$. A node enters
 \f$ R \f$ once, and a cursor in \f$ \mathrm{adj}( j ) \f$ never goes
 back; hence each node is taken \f$ O( 1 + d( j ) ) \f$ times, each edge is
 examined once from its tail, and a visit costs \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$
 time on lists. At the end \f$ R \f$ holds the nodes reachable from the
 root, and \f$ B \f$ is a tree that spans them, with one edge less than
 \f$ R \f$ (an invariant of the loop, checked at the level of the audits).
 Taking the oldest node of \f$ Q \f$ gives the visit in breadth, and taking
 the newest the visit in depth.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the visit in breadth).</em> With \f$ Q \f$ a queue,
 \f$ d[ v ] = d[ u ] + 1 \f$ set when \f$ v \f$ is reached from \f$ u \f$, and
 \f$ d[ r ] = 0 \f$, one has at the end
 \f[
   d[ v ] = \delta( r , v ) \quad \forall v \in V . \tag{1}
 \f]

 Indeed, the values of \f$ d \f$ in the queue never decrease from its head to
 its tail and differ by at most 1. A node at distance \f$ k \f$ is therefore
 dequeued before any node at distance \f$ k + 1 \f$ is reached, and by
 induction on \f$ k \f$ a node \f$ v \f$ with \f$ \delta( r , v ) = k + 1 \f$
 is first reached from a node \f$ u \f$ at distance \f$ k \f$, which gives (1).
 Hence `distance()` of `Visit.h` gives the shortest paths in edges in
 \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$, and its tree of the parents is a tree of shortest
 paths. The same argument holds with several sources at distance 0
 (`multiSourceDistance()` of `Paths.h` and `gridDistances()` of `Grid.h`).
 Also, `countShortestPaths()` of `Paths.h` adds to the count of \f$ v \f$ that
 of each \f$ u \f$ with \f$ d[ v ] = d[ u ] + 1 \f$, and by the same order of
 the levels the count is complete when \f$ v \f$ is dequeued.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the visit in depth).</em> In a visit in depth the intervals
 \f$ [ \mathit{dt}[ u ] , \mathit{ft}[ u ] ] \f$ of two nodes are either
 disjoint or nested, and \f$ v \f$ descends from \f$ u \f$ in the forest of
 the visit if and only if its interval lies in that of \f$ u \f$.
 Classifying an edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ when it is examined, as `DfsSchema` of
 `Visit.h` does, gives
 \f[
   \begin{array}{ll}
   \text{tree} & \mathit{dt}[ v ] = 0 , \cr
   \text{back} & \mathit{dt}[ u ] > \mathit{dt}[ v ] \text{ and }
                 \mathit{ft}[ v ] = 0 , \cr
   \text{forward} & \mathit{dt}[ u ] < \mathit{dt}[ v ] \text{ and }
                    \mathit{ft}[ v ] \ne 0 , \cr
   \text{cross} & \text{otherwise,}
   \end{array} \tag{2}
 \f]
 and an oriented graph has a cycle if and only if a visit finds a back edge.

 In fact, the nesting holds since the visit of \f$ v \f$ either starts and ends
 while \f$ u \f$ is on the stack, or does not start before \f$ u \f$ is
 finished. A back edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ closes a cycle with the path of the
 tree from \f$ v \f$ to \f$ u \f$. Conversely, if \f$ v \f$ is the first node
 of a cycle to be discovered, the other nodes of the cycle are reached before
 \f$ v \f$ is finished (the path of white nodes of Tarjan); the edge of the
 cycle that enters \f$ v \f$ is then examined while \f$ v \f$ is on the stack,
 i.e., it is a back edge. Note that on an unoriented graph each edge, met again
 from its other end, is a back edge in the sense of (2), and `hasCycle()`
 returns true as soon as the graph has an edge.

 Next, the finish times give a topological order. If the graph is acyclic,
 each edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ has
 \f[
   \mathit{ft}[ v ] < \mathit{ft}[ u ] , \tag{3}
 \f]
 since by (2) it is a tree, forward or cross edge, and \f$ v \f$ is finished
 before \f$ u \f$ in all three cases; hence the nodes by decreasing finish
 time are in a topological order (`topsort()`). The algorithm of Kahn
 (`topsortKahn()`) reaches the same goal by removing, at each step, a node with
 no entering edge, which an acyclic graph always has. With the counts of the
 entering edges kept in a vector it costs \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$, and it
 stops with fewer than \f$ n \f$ nodes if and only if a cycle remains. The
 topological order is unique if and only if the set of the sources never
 holds two nodes (`uniqueTopologicalOrder()` of `Properties.h`), i.e., if and
 only if consecutive nodes of the order are joined by an edge.

 <em>Lemma 3 (Kosaraju).</em> Let \f$ C \f$ and \f$ C' \f$ be two strongly
 connected components, and \f$ \mathit{ft}( C ) \f$ the largest finish time of
 a node of \f$ C \f$ in a visit in depth of \f$ G \f$. If an edge goes from
 \f$ C \f$ to \f$ C' \f$, then \f$ \mathit{ft}( C ) > \mathit{ft}( C' ) \f$
 (Theorem 9.1 of the book).

 Therefore, a visit of the transposed graph \f$ G^T \f$ started from the
 node of largest finish time stays in its component, since an edge that
 leaves it in \f$ G^T \f$ can only enter a component already visited; the
 next visit, from the largest finish time left, does the same. Thus, `scc()`
 of `Visit.h` costs two visits, each \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$. The graph of
 the components is acyclic, and `unilaterallyConnected()` tests whether its
 topological order is unique.

 \section theory_graph_sp Shortest paths

 With a cost \f$ w( u , v ) \f$ on each edge and a source \f$ r \f$, a tree
 \f$ T \f$ rooted in \f$ r \f$, with \f$ d[ v ] \f$ the cost of its path from
 \f$ r \f$ to \f$ v \f$, is a tree of shortest paths if and only if (theorem
 of Bellman, Theorem 11.1 of the book)
 \f[
   d[ v ] = d[ u ] + w( u , v ) \quad \forall ( u , v ) \in T , \qquad
   d[ u ] + w( u , v ) \ge d[ v ] \quad \forall ( u , v ) \in E , \tag{4}
 \f]
 provided that no cycle of negative cost is reachable from \f$ r \f$ (with
 one, no shortest path exists). The prototype of section 11.2
 (`shortestPaths()` of `ShortestPaths.h`) starts from \f$ d[ r ] = 0 \f$ and
 \f$ d[ v ] = \infty \f$ elsewhere and, while an edge violates (4), sets
 \f$ d[ v ] = d[ u ] + w( u , v ) \f$ and makes \f$ u \f$ the parent of
 \f$ v \f$; a structure \f$ S \f$ holds the nodes whose edges have to be
 checked again. Each \f$ d[ v ] \f$ is at all times the cost of some path
 from \f$ r \f$, and it only decreases; hence, when \f$ S \f$ is empty, (4)
 holds and \f$ d \f$ is optimal. The kind of \f$ S \f$ decides how many
 times a node is extracted, and therefore the cost.

 <table>
 <caption>The structure \f$ S \f$ of the prototype and its cost</caption>
 <tr><th>\f$ S \f$</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Time</th>
     <th>Negative costs</th></tr>
 <tr><td>priority queue on an unsorted list</td><td>Dijkstra</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n^2 ) \f$</td><td>correct, a node possibly extracted more
     than once</td></tr>
 <tr><td>binary heap</td><td>Johnson</td><td>\f$ O( m \log n ) \f$</td>
     <td>as above</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Fibonacci heap</td><td>Fredman and Tarjan</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m + n \log n ) \f$</td><td>as above</td></tr>
 <tr><td>queue</td><td>Bellman, Ford and Moore</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n m ) \f$</td><td>a negative cycle detected</td></tr>
 <tr><td>stack, deque</td><td>Pape and D'Esopo (deque)</td>
     <td>exponential in the worst case</td><td>no negative cycle
     allowed</td></tr>
 </table>

 With nonnegative costs and \f$ S \f$ a priority queue on \f$ d \f$, the
 node \f$ u \f$ extracted has \f$ d[ u ] = \delta( r , u ) \f$: any other
 path to \f$ u \f$ leaves the extracted nodes through a node of \f$ S \f$
 of priority at least \f$ d[ u ] \f$, and goes on with costs that are not
 negative. Hence each node is extracted once and each edge is examined once,
 and the time is that of \f$ n \f$ extractions and of at most \f$ m \f$
 decreases of a priority, i.e., \f$ n \cdot O( n ) + m \cdot O( 1 ) \f$
 on the unsorted list, \f$ ( n + m ) \cdot O( \log n ) \f$ on the binary
 heap and \f$ n \cdot O( \log n ) + m \cdot O( 1 ) \f$ amortized on the
 Fibonacci heap. With a queue, the nodes extracted in the \f$ k \f$-th
 round (those that entered \f$ S \f$ in the round before) have their final
 distance if one of their shortest paths has at most \f$ k \f$ edges. A
 shortest path has at most \f$ n - 1 \f$ edges; hence each node leaves the
 queue at most \f$ n \f$ times, the time is \f$ O( n m ) \f$, and a node
 extracted more than \f$ n \f$ times proves that a negative cycle is
 reachable (exercise 11.2). On an acyclic graph one can do better, whatever
 the signs of the costs: the scan of the nodes in topological order relaxes
 the edges of each node once, after all the edges that enter it, and (4)
 holds in \f$ O( n + m ) \f$ (`dagShortestPaths()`; the longest paths of
 `dagLongestPaths()` of `Paths.h` follow the same recurrence with the maximum).

 Between all the pairs of nodes, with \f$ d^k[ u ][ v ] \f$ the least cost
 of a path from \f$ u \f$ to \f$ v \f$ whose inner nodes are among the first
 \f$ k \f$, one has (section 13.5)
 \f[
   d^k[ u ][ v ] = \min\{ d^{k-1}[ u ][ v ] ,
                          d^{k-1}[ u ][ k ] + d^{k-1}[ k ][ v ] \} ,
   \tag{5}
 \f]
 since such a path either avoids the node \f$ k \f$ or passes through it
 once (no cycle being negative); `floydWarshall()` of `AllPairsShortestPaths.h`
 fills (5) in place, in \f$ \Theta( n^3 ) \f$ time and
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ space, and a negative cycle shows as
 \f$ d[ u ][ u ] < 0 \f$. After the insertion of an edge \f$ ( a , b ) \f$
 a path that improves uses it once, and the update
 \f$ d[ u ][ v ] \gets \min\{ d[ u ][ v ] , d[ u ][ a ] + w + d[ b ][ v ] \}
 \f$ costs \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ (`addEdge()`); a deletion has no such update.

 \section theory_graph_mst Minimum spanning trees

 On an unoriented connected graph a spanning tree has \f$ n - 1 \f$ edges,
 and a minimum one has the least total weight. Both algorithms of
 `MinimumSpanningTree.h` rest on the property of the cuts (Theorem 14.1 of the
 book), which reads as follows: if \f$ A \f$ is contained in a minimum
 spanning tree and no edge of \f$ A \f$ crosses the cut
 \f$ ( S , V \setminus S ) \f$, then
 \f[
   A \cup \{ e \} \text{ is contained in a minimum spanning tree, }
   e \text{ a lightest edge across } ( S , V \setminus S ) . \tag{6}
 \f]
 Indeed, in a minimum tree that does not contain \f$ e \f$ the path between
 the ends of \f$ e \f$ crosses the cut on some edge \f$ e' \f$ with
 \f$ w( e ) \le w( e' ) \f$, and swapping \f$ e' \f$ for \f$ e \f$ gives
 another spanning tree that is no heavier.

 `kruskal()` takes the edges by increasing weight and keeps one if its ends are
 in two components of the forest built so far, i.e., if it is a lightest
 edge across the cut around one of them. The sort costs
 \f$ O( m \log n ) \f$ (or \f$ O( m + n + k ) \f$ by counting, with integer
 weights in \f$ [ 0 , k ) \f$), and with an MFSET with the rank and the
 compression of the paths the rest costs \f$ O( m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) \f$,
 \f$ \alpha \f$ the inverse of the function of Ackermann, which is at most
 4 in practice. `prim()` grows one tree from \f$ r \f$, the cut being the tree
 itself, and keeps the nodes out of it in a priority queue by the weight of
 their lightest edge to it; this gives the same three bounds as the shortest
 paths (\f$ O( n^2 ) \f$, \f$ O( m \log n ) \f$ and
 \f$ O( m + n \log n ) \f$), the list being usually preferable on the dense
 graphs. Dually, a spanning forest is minimum if and only if no edge out of
 it weighs less than the heaviest edge of the path of the forest between its
 ends (the property of the cycles), and `isMinimumSpanningForest()` checks it
 in \f$ O( m n ) \f$.

 \section theory_graph_flow Flows, cuts and matchings

 A network has a source \f$ s \f$, a sink \f$ t \f$ and a capacity
 \f$ c( u , v ) \ge 0 \f$ on each edge; a flow satisfies
 \f$ 0 \le f( u , v ) \le c( u , v ) \f$ and is conserved at every node but
 \f$ s \f$ and \f$ t \f$, and its value \f$ | f | \f$ is the net flow out of
 \f$ s \f$. `FlowNetwork` of `MaxFlow.h` keeps for each edge a twin in the
 opposite direction, with \f$ f( v , u ) = - f( u , v ) \f$, so that the
 residual network, made of the edges with \f$ c_f( u , v ) > 0 \f$, is at hand.

 <em>Theorem 4 (maximum flow, minimum cut).</em> The following are equivalent:
 (i) \f$ f \f$ is a maximum flow; (ii) the residual network has no path from
 \f$ s \f$ to \f$ t \f$ (an augmenting path); (iii)
 \f$ | f | = c( S , V \setminus S ) \f$ for some cut with \f$ s \in S \f$ and
 \f$ t \notin S \f$ (Theorem 15.1 of the book).

 A flow is bounded by the capacity of any cut, and hence (iii) gives (i);
 (i) gives (ii), since an augmenting path would increase the flow by its
 least residual capacity. For (ii) to (iii), the nodes \f$ S \f$ reachable
 from \f$ s \f$ in the residual network give a cut whose edges are
 saturated and whose reverse edges carry no flow (`minCut()`).

 The algorithms augment along augmenting paths until (ii) holds, and differ in
 the path chosen. With integer capacities each augmentation adds at least 1;
 hence any path (`FORD_FULKERSON`, by a visit in depth) gives
 \f$ O( ( n + m ) | f^* | ) \f$, a bound that depends on the values of the
 capacities and may be poor when they are large with respect to \f$ n \f$ and
 \f$ m \f$. A shortest path in edges (`EDMONDS_KARP`, by a visit in breadth)
 never decreases the distances of the residual network. Each edge is then the
 critical edge of an augmentation at most \f$ n / 2 \f$ times, since between
 two such times the distance of its tail grows by 2; there are therefore
 \f$ O( n m ) \f$ augmentations of \f$ O( m ) \f$ each, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n m^2 ) \f$. `DINIC` sends at once a blocking flow along all the
 shortest paths of the levels of a visit in breadth, and each such phase
 increases the distance of \f$ t \f$. At most \f$ n \f$ phases of
 \f$ O( n m ) \f$ give \f$ O( n^2 m ) \f$, and the bound falls to
 \f$ O( m \sqrt{n} ) \f$ on the networks of unit capacities (Even and Tarjan),
 e.g., those of the bipartite matching; in practice the phases are usually far
 fewer than \f$ n \f$.

 The flow found is integral when the capacities are (the integrality theorem),
 and this makes three reductions exact. The edge-disjoint paths of
 `edgeDisjointPaths()` of `FlowApplications.h` are as many as the maximum flow
 with unit capacities (the theorem of Menger). The b-matching of
 `bipartiteBMatching()` of `Matching.h` is a maximum flow from a source joined
 to the left side to a sink joined from the right one. Finally, a circulation
 with lower bounds (`feasibleCirculation()`) exists if and only if, after the
 lower bounds are sent, a maximum flow from a new source to a new sink balances
 the excess left at each node.

 \section theory_graph_euler Eulerian cycles and other properties

 By the theorem of Euler, an oriented graph has a closed path that uses each
 edge exactly once if and only if every node has as many entering edges as
 leaving ones and the edges lie in one component, the orientation ignored.
 Under these conditions a walk along unused edges can only stop at its
 start, and the algorithm of Hierholzer (`eulerianCycle()` of
 `EulerianCycle.h`) splices into the walk the closed walks found from its
 nodes, with a stack, in \f$ O( n + m ) \f$, since each edge is followed
 once.

 Several other headers decide properties by the visits above, with the bound of
 a visit unless their comments say otherwise. A free tree has \f$ m = n - 1 \f$
 edges and one component (`isTree()`). A graph is bipartite if and only if it
 has no odd cycle (the theorem of Koenig), i.e., if and only if no edge of a
 visit in breadth joins two nodes of levels of the same parity
 (`twoColoring()`). In a free tree the node farthest from any node ends a
 longest path, and the node farthest from it ends the other (the double sweep
 of `treeDiameter()`). The triangles are counted in \f$ O( m \sqrt{m} ) \f$ by
 orienting each edge towards the end of larger degree, which leaves each node
 with \f$ O( \sqrt{m} ) \f$ out-neighbours (`countTriangles()`). Finally, the
 algorithm of Johnson lists the simple cycles through a node in
 \f$ O( ( n + m ) ( c + 1 ) ) \f$ for \f$ c \f$ cycles (`simpleCycles()` of
 `Paths.h`), since it keeps the search from entering a branch with no cycle at
 its end; the backtracking of chapter 16 may instead spend exponential time
 between two cycles.

